{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfpoLtW3Wcz2htfF75KB4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The framing I'd push back on first: \"done\" and \"needs a human\" aren't two ends of one scale. They're answers to different questions, and conflating them causes both failure modes. A task can be fully done *and* still need a human call — the ambiguity got resolved by assumption and the assumption should be reviewed. A task can be incomplete and need no human at all; it just needs more work.\n\nSo I'd test them separately.\n\n**For \"done\":** every claim in the request maps to an artifact I actually verified, and the scope is the stated scope. The trap is that \"tests pass\" is weaker evidence than it looks when I wrote the tests. The suite and the implementation share a single point of failure — my reading of the spec. If I misread \"non-alphanumeric,\" I'd write tests that encode the misreading and an implementation that satisfies them, and get a green run that proves only self-consistency. That's the strongest argument for your tests-first instruction, better than the usual TDD one: it gets my interpretation in front of you *before* an implementation anchors to it.\n\n**For \"needs a human\":** the trigger isn't that I feel uncertain, it's that the request is *underdetermined* — two careful engineers reading the same text would produce different artifacts — plus at least one of:\n\n1. the missing information lives outside the repo (I can't close the gap with more effort),\n2. being wrong is expensive to reverse, or\n3. it changes what the thing is *for*, not just how it's built.\n\nUnderdetermination alone means decide, flag, and keep going. Underdetermination plus one of those means ask.\n\nThat reproduces what I did an hour ago: non-ASCII hit all three — what slugs are *for* (URLs? filenames? anchor IDs?) isn't derivable from a repo containing one `trim` function — so I asked. Underscore-as-separator was underdetermined too, but the issue's literal wording broke the tie and the fix is one character in a regex, so I picked and flagged it.\n\nThough let me turn that on myself, because criterion 2 is the one I'm least able to evaluate from inside the task. Reversal cost depends on downstream consumers I can't see. If slugs get persisted as URL keys, changing the algorithm later breaks every existing link and my \"one character\" fix becomes a migration. Slugs are persistence-shaped. So the honest rule is probably: *unknown* reversal cost in a persistence-shaped feature should push toward asking, which makes the underscore call closer than I presented it.\n\nThe practical upshot is that most of this shouldn't route through asking at all. The real question usually isn't \"ask or guess\" but \"can I make this decision cheap to reverse and visible to a reviewer, then proceed.\" A debatable call parked in a test case is legible and costs one line to change. The same call buried in a regex is invisible. Escalation is also a budget — ask about six things and you stop reading the questions carefully, so the one that mattered gets rubber-stamped.\n\nAnd timing matters more than the ask itself: a question raised after everything else is finished, presented as a fork, costs you almost nothing. The same question raised up front, blocking, costs you the whole task.\n\nWhere I'm least sure: whether \"the information lives outside the repo\" is doing real work, or whether it's mostly a cover for not having searched hard enough. Does that match what you've seen go wrong?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10608,"cache_read_input_tokens":12800,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10608},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_t